        "body_paragraph_zero_based": 390,
        "direct_body_paragraph_zero_based": 340,
        "paragraph_id": "25F49A41",
        "text": "The evaluation of the term , the so-called third even velocity moment, depends on the situation. First we need expressions for the orbital velocity amplitude, which is expressed as:"
      }
    ],
    "following_nonempty_paragraphs": [
      {
        "container_path": "models/XBeach/raw/source_code/trunk/doc/manual/XBeach_manual_master.docx",
        "member": "word/document.xml",
        "start_xml_ordinal": 14538,
        "body_paragraph_zero_based": 394,
        "direct_body_paragraph_zero_based": 344,
        "paragraph_id": "6CB3AB3B",
        "text": "In this formulation Tp is the peak wave period, Hrms is the root-mean-square wave height, k is the wave number and h is the local water depth."
      },
      {
        "container_path": "models/XBeach/raw/source_code/trunk/doc/manual/XBeach_manual_master.docx",
        "member": "word/document.xml",
        "start_xml_ordinal": 14598,
        "body_paragraph_zero_based": 396,
        "direct_body_paragraph_zero_based": 346,
        "paragraph_id": "4309A011",
        "text": "If we consider the slowly-varying dissipation in wave groups, we need only to average over a single wave period and we can use a monochromatic (regular wave) expression. If we want to have the time-average dissipation over a full spectrum we get the best approximation from considering a linear Gaussian distribution. Guza and Thornton (1985) give pragmatic expressions for both cases."
      }
    ],
    "table_cell": null,
    "text_method": "Saved display-field substitution plus w:t; equation objects are not transcribed."
